        <item>
            <title>0.10.0</title>
            <pubDate>Thu, 10 Sep 2026 17:17:13 +0200</pubDate>
            <sparkle:version>1000</sparkle:version>
            <sparkle:shortVersionString>0.10.0</sparkle:shortVersionString>
            <sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>14.0</sparkle:minimumSystemVersion>
            <sparkle:hardwareRequirements>arm64</sparkle:hardwareRequirements>
            <description><![CDATA[<h2>Jarvis 0.10.0</h2>
<p><b>Priorities.</b> Every task now carries one of four levels &mdash; urgent, high,
normal, low &mdash; that Jarvis works out from who is asking, whether you started
the thread, and the deadline. You can override it, and Jarvis learns from your
overrides.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The TODAY list is sorted by priority; the checkbox ring shows the level
      (red = urgent, amber = high). Right-click a task to change it.</li>
  <li>In a task, a chip like &ldquo;High &middot; Jarvis&rdquo; shows the level and who
      decided it. Click it to pin a level with a note, or let Jarvis decide again.
      Your choices are fed back into Jarvis as standing instructions.</li>
  <li>Slack profile titles (CEO, Head of Growth&hellip;) now reach Jarvis so a
      request from someone senior ranks higher.</li>
  <li>Jarvis opens on your TODAY list.</li>
  <li>Fixed: a rare state where marking a task done or dismissing it silently did
      nothing until you restarted Jarvis.</li>
  <li>Fixed: a task Jarvis found could be lost when its answer came back
      malformed; it now retries on the next check instead of waiting an hour.</li>
  <li>Coding agents: <code>list_tasks</code> now includes <code>priority</code> and
      <code>prioritySource</code>.</li>
  <li>No change to what leaves your Mac: Slack is still searched live and never
      stored; only titles, levels and your own notes go into the prompts.</li>
</ul>
]]></description>
